WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Dragon Warrior, the epic beginning of a new era in video games.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:28.300
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.940
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.160
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.400
You know, the epic beginning of a new era in video games is a pretty ballsy take for anybody.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:46.140
But JRPGs are a huge part of the, you know, video game atmosphere and it's still around today and everything.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:54.640
And this is the first one we've been talking about Dragon Warrior for a long time on the show, just like the build up to it and how excited we are to play a JRPG on the NES.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.300
And I think that's, maybe the epic beginning of a new era in video games isn't too much hyperbole here.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.920
I was gonna ask, like, is, so that was on the original box?

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.560
Like that's not a reissue or something that's been said recently?

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.560
That was just when this came out?

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.400
That is the big bold letters on the back of the box.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.700
Incredible. Wow.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.360
Yeah, a lot of foresight there.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:23.300
I would say it's a, I would just say it's kind of, you know, like narcissistic, but you know, maybe that too.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:33.560
I think at this point though, because of the way, you know, it was going in Japan with releases, I don't even know what Dragon Quest game they were up to in on the Famicom by now.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:41.940
But it was already like such a big deal in Japan that they even had like people taking the day off to play the new Dragon Quest game.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:53.880
So maybe they thought like the success of Super Mario Brothers doing so well in Japan and then also doing so well in North America was like going to happen again for Dragon Quest to Dragon Warrior.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.180
Yeah, I can see that.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.840
RPG like sprawling world adventure.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.760
But it's not exactly like fully there.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:34.300
So I think it is a, you know, it is an interesting thing to look back on where they're saying like, this is the beginning of a new era.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.780
And then like it almost, it almost coincidentally became the beginning of a new era.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:49.760
Yeah, I think like, you know, for our generation guys, like, you know, Pokemon Red and Blue in a really weird way was the introduction to our generation of like turn-based battles.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:50.980
Not necessarily JRPGs.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.080
JRPGs. I wouldn't even call Pokemon a JRPG.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:59.880
But like some tropes of that, of the video game mechanics and definitely, you know, turn-based battles and that idea.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:09.940
But, you know, that was like the gateway to like, okay, well, I played Pokemon Red and then like, you know, you might discover Final Fantasy as a result of that or other, you know, Nintendo-related RPG style games.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.840
Dragon Quest is not quite the gateway.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:19.760
It's actually like the beginner's book, you know, it just creates a lot of these JRPG mechanics for the very first time.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.120
And they're still around today.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:28.160
And yeah, they are basic concepts that now like every game just like, it's not even something you think about putting in.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.020
You just put it in because that's how the battle systems and the world mechanics are designed.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:45.380
But, you know, you've got turn-based battles, leveling up, you know, the idea and thought of grinding, being able to save your progress, using magic, exploring a large map.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.340
Joe, you kind of said that, you know, we did see this with even just like Ultima, right?

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:54.480
Like that was a Western version of an RPG, but it's not the first time we're seeing this stuff.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:04.900
I think it's just important to note that like Dragon Warrior is doing it in a way where it's, you know, so basic that it actually allows anybody to get in and figure it all out, right?

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.140
It's not as complex.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:18.280
Definitely, definitely. And also just something about this, I mean, looking at it from, you know, from the present day perspective, this does feel more like what I'm used to in a JRPG than Ultima Exodus did.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.800
Oh, yeah. I mean, this is a very comfortable game for me.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:33.960
Like it's a lot of the same concepts that I grew up on and you can tell that like not much has really changed here in terms of basic JRPG design.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:50.640
But in terms of Ultima, like that sort of felt like this really weird, like bizarro game where there were some aspects that I recognized, but they were all sort of like through this weird filter of not quite D&D, not quite JRPG.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:05:00.400
Whereas this is like a very clear lineage to where I started playing these kinds of games.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.400
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:07.580
It feels like it's much more refined in that sense, whereas Ultima felt like it was like not.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:13.600
It was something. Like they didn't understand how to make it, you know, like they understand what they wanted.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.860
They didn't understand how it would best work on a video game console.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:24.280
Yeah. Yeah, you could say that this streamlines a lot of adventure aspects that you would find in even tabletop gaming.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:32.880
And, you know, some people would say that some of the features in this game now are, you know, even obtuse for the time.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:42.260
Like being able to have like an action command for stairs so that you go down the stairs or not just pressing the A button for the treasure chest.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:44.240
Like you actually have to say take.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:58.460
These are things that like it's both establishing these ideas and concepts, but also taking a step back from modern – not modern games, but modern games of that time in 1989.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:02.380
Like things that we're more familiar with in other games that aren't RPGs.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:12.440
Yeah, I'm not really sure if I would consider that like a breakthrough or something to build upon, but I'm sure we'll get into that.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.900
Well, you're talking about the command menu still?

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:15.880
Yes. Yeah.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:26.580
I mean, it is like – I'm trying to remember exactly how Ultima worked, not to keep comparing it to Ultima, but like it is like a refinement in the sense that like the things you can do are much less.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:35.320
And like, yeah, it's just really weird that you have to select the stairs command to walk upstairs that you're already standing on.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.340
Like what else are you going to do to the stairs?

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:48.320
But at least it never got like so complicated where I was like – where I was thinking, so what do I do with this?

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.760
It was always just like one extra step than what I'm used to to interact with anything.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:07:07.440
But would either of you be able to pretend for a minute that you didn't play the game, right, or that you aren't familiar with, you know, let's say 20 to 30 years of various JRPG knowledge?

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:15.540
If you just like came into the world of Dragon Warrior, maybe peruse the manual a little bit but didn't like sit down with it as a guidebook.

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:24.200
When you start up this game and you're given the assignment and you're locked in that room and there's the three treasure chests, right?

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:33.560
The idea of like how the game presents the information to you, you know, we're talking about like Legend of Zelda and how they let you go without the sword, right?

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:37.600
Like it's up to you to go into that very obvious cave and collect the sword.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:43.100
I'd argue that Dragon Warrior kind of does the same thing with the idea that nobody has ever done this before.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:52.320
So we need to kind of like show them without directly saying like, hey, you know, there's a key in this door and you can, you know, press the take button and then press the door button.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:54.740
You know, like they just – they let you figure these things out this way.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.380
You understand how the mechanics work.

00:07:56.380 --> 00:08:02.060
But even just like random encounters isn't something that like there's no tutorial.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:03.380
There's no spelling it out for you.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:09.180
You just find out like, oh, I got to battle to grind, to level up, to get stronger, to battle other enemies.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.480
You know, what do you think about that introduction?

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.640
Yeah, I think it does do some of the same things.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:31.060
But in some cases it almost does like the opposite where Legend of Zelda puts you on that first screen and expects that the player will just be curious enough to explore and find these things that teach them.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:40.200
Whereas Dragon Warrior locks you in a room until you do these couple of tasks that will teach you how to do those tasks throughout the rest of the game.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:43.040
So it's like a little bit of a different mindset.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:53.240
That's the complete opposite. Like that is – you have to cross this threshold whereas like something like Zelda will let you figure it out on your own.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:59.000
Where this one – it's quite literally a gate that you have to unlock.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:00.740
Yeah. Well, exactly.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:13.780
But I was going to say that the – to address what you were saying, Mike, about the random encounters, that's where I do feel like it feels a little more Zelda-like where like that is just like you'll just learn.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:25.720
I mean I guess you'll also still in that case now that I think about it have no choice but to kind of figure it out in that battle or die as serious as that sounds.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:36.960
But yeah, you'll just decide like okay, well I guess I'll attack and you'll see that you do damage and it'll just teach you rather than like it literally teaching you.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:45.240
Yeah, and you can go as far as you really want to in the opening map, much to your own peril of course.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:53.560
But if you don't even choose to just like stay near the castle and just fight the nearby enemies, there's a lot – I mean yes, some of it's locked off.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.080
But there's a lot of places you can cross the bridge all the way to the other continent.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:03.220
You can go all the way up to the northwest and go to the other town up there in the desert.

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:04.980
You can explore a cave.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:10.820
Like there's nothing that says exactly what you need to do next.

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:16.180
It's up to you to talk to the NPCs and get the clues.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.920
And yes, they're not like riddles or anything.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:23.500
They are kind of hitting you over the head with where to – even just like what direction to walk in.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:37.860
But I think there's something to that too, comparing it again to Legend of Zelda, just like the scale of the game impressed me because it wasn't just like go to this town, do this thing, go to the next town.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:43.260
Like a lot of it was spent trying to figure out like when is it time to move on?

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:44.680
When do I feel comfortable moving on?

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:50.780
Should I grind to gain a bunch of gold to buy this sword or am I comfortable with my club?

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:52.360
Like can I move on without it?

00:10:52.360 --> 00:11:03.480
Yeah, because I think a lot of games that we're used to in this like JRPG setting, like they're all sort of story gates.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:12.740
Like you're supposed to hit this part of the story and then do this thing, whereas this very much is just level gated.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:17.640
Like you have to grind and grind and grind and it doesn't matter who you talk to.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:21.140
I mean you should be talking to people to get an idea of where key items are.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:28.240
But where you should be is entirely just based on what level you're at.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:36.240
And so there isn't that information of like, okay, I hit this story beat.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:40.180
So now it's time to go to this island or this dungeon.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:49.100
You just sort of have to prod at the wilderness and see are these enemies that I can handle?

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:55.820
And if they are, then maybe – and they're a little bit more difficult than I'm used to, but I can still handle them.

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.500
Maybe this is where I'm supposed to go.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:02.060
So it's a completely different way of thinking about progression.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:07.540
I guess sort of in a weird way.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:12.720
It's simpler, but I had to sort of unlearn what I had learned in other games.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:32.380
Yeah, I think that my favorite type of progression in a JRPG like this, particularly thinking of like the PS1 Final Fantasy games or even the Super Nintendo Final Fantasy games, where you have that experience where you are not sure if you're powerful enough to move on.

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:51.020
Even if you have the story information where you know where to go, and rather than just saying, okay, well, I better stand here and grind and go to this forest and fight a bunch of enemies, there are other things to do in the world that give you that experience of grinding without feeling like you're just exclusively grinding.

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:52.300
It feels like you're exploring the world.

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:53.820
It feels like you're still playing the game.

00:12:54.820 --> 00:13:12.580
And this Dragon Warrior did what I would consider like half of that in the sense that it gave you the need to grind and feel like I can explore the world, but it didn't really have a lot to do that felt satisfying outside of the actual battles that weren't complete progression.

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:14.520
Yeah, there's no explicit side quests.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:20.600
It's just sort of you fumbling around and not really knowing where to go, and then you end up grinding.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:22.800
Even though in the manual it tells you to grind.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:25.140
Right. As soon as you start.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:29.900
I mean, you know, I think we can get out of the way right now that grinding is a huge part of this game.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:32.300
I thought there was no avoiding that.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:38.820
Joe, that's an interesting segue, though, too, about just the scale of the game and what happens in the game, right?

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:43.260
If we just focus on, you know, not the story beats, right?

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:47.100
Not how the story evolves, but just how you play the game.

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:50.860
Does this really feel like a game where anything could happen?

00:13:50.860 --> 00:14:00.880
Like, the size of the game itself, like, the map is bigger than what we're used to, and, you know, you go to a lot of different towns, and there are caves to explore and stuff like that.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:10.800
But once you, like, get through, like, let's say the first hour of the game, were you still thinking that you could be surprised, or were you maybe a little worried that you had seen it all?

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:26.120
I mean, I definitely, I can't really remember how I was feeling in that moment, but looking back, I mean, I definitely found, I guess it depends on what you're asking by, like, surprises.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:32.860
I found things that would surprise me a little bit later in the game where it's like, oh, this type of enemy is stronger.

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:37.100
I haven't seen this type of enemy before, which is a minor, a minor thing.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:42.820
And also stuff like, you know, you get, at one point you get a, like, a reverse pokey flute.

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:44.760
You get a flute that will put people to sleep.

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:50.240
And I don't know if it only puts the one enemy that I ended up using it on to sleep, or if you can use it on other enemies.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:58.100
But, like, things like that did end up, or items that I could buy that were useful, spells that I would gain that would give me different abilities.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:04.400
That turned out to be, at least some of them, surprisingly useful in and outside of battle.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:10.920
So, like, that was a little more than I expected from this very early JRPG.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:14.980
I expected the spells to be kind of basic and not do much.

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:16.740
And there were a few that would pop up.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.200
Yeah. Yeah, go on.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:28.080
I mean, like, to answer Mike's question as well, like, I, after an hour, figured, like, no, this is, this is the game.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:38.100
And, yeah, there's going to be new enemies and bigger bosses or, and a couple different, like, story snippets.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:40.380
But I was pretty sure that this was it.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:44.360
Like, the base combat doesn't really change.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:48.700
There's, it's just attack, run, use item.

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:52.080
There's some magic with, and a couple of them have status effects.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.420
Nothing really gets more complex as you go on.

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:58.700
It's an, it's a, a magnitude of numbers game.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:01.540
Like, if you have a higher level, you're going to beat things.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:04.320
There's no, like, rock, paper, scissors, elemental.

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:12.080
Any of these, like, very basic tropes that have grown out of standard RPGs over the years.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:14.420
Like, it, it just is what it is.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:18.200
And, like, more things happen.

00:16:18.200 --> 00:16:21.180
But there's nothing that really surprised me, like you were saying.

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:23.340
Aside from a couple things at the end.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:30.260
Right. And I think that's what I was getting at, too, is just, you know, this is, these aren't knocks against this game because it's such an early game.

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:34.420
But in other JRPGs, you might acquire a new party member.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.620
You are the only, there is no party in this game.

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:39.320
You're the sole warrior that takes on the quest.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:48.860
Or you might learn a new technique that opens up a new menu option in your combat where it's not just attack or spell.

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:52.080
You know? You might change your class or something like that.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:54.300
These things don't happen, so the surprises aren't there.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:56.040
Even just, like, overworld surprises.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:02.400
You know, in Final Fantasy, there's always the trope of different vehicles becoming your method of traveling throughout the world.

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:05.420
And in this one, you're just on foot for the entirety of the game.

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:15.820
So a lot of the game that's impressive right at the start, you know, is like, wow, this is a big step in the, you know, NES when you explore the games like how we are chronologically.

00:17:15.820 --> 00:17:27.400
But at the same time, once you realize what this game is, a lot of it is just, like Sean was saying, a numbers game where you're going to the new town or going back to an old town to get something.

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:37.380
And in the meantime, you are just constantly, constantly going through random encounters to do almost like an idle clicker.

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:43.800
Like a cookie clicker game and stuff like that, where you are just watching the numbers on the left-hand side of your screen.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:49.760
They just count up and you're just like, cool, once I hit this number, I'll be satisfied.

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:53.420
And then, you know, if you're like me, you all of a sudden start changing the number.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:58.100
You're like, oh, well, that other number is getting closer to a nice rounder number too, so I'll just hang out a little longer.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:17:59.740
But that's besides the point.

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:23.540
I think it's time that we actually do talk about the combat here, you know, and just how these battles work because it is a little different than even, you know, most JRPGs that we're used to because of the one-on-one nature of these battles.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:28.080
Yeah, I mean, it's still very familiar.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:32.820
Like you have your attack, magic list, escape, and item.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:40.760
But the only things that can really change in terms of like turn order are like you can get up.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:49.200
You can be attacked preemptively, but usually you're going to go first and then just going to go you, them, you, them.

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:56.600
And it's really just keep your HP up and still do some damage.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.560
Yeah, it's pretty simple.

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:06.660
You can also have them skip turns by putting them to sleep and they can just sort of do the same to you with like time stop and stuff like that.

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:12.200
If you've played any turn-based RPG, you've played a version of this.

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:21.240
Yeah, I would say that there's probably not as much strategy involved compared to really any other JRPG.

00:19:21.340 --> 00:19:26.300
You know, it's like the RPG battle system boiled down to its like most basic form.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:29.540
So it really is just do I attack?

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.620
Do I use my like one damaging spell?

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:35.520
Do I use a status effect or do I heal?

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:37.340
And like that's it.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:48.700
And the fact that you don't have other party members really just makes it where you can't think like, okay, I'm going to have my warrior do this while my thief does that while my healer does this.

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:53.060
So I can, you know, really plan out how I want to time things out.

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:54.760
This is just what are you doing this turn?

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:55.940
Okay, what are you doing this turn?

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:57.120
Just a race to zero.

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:58.640
Yeah. Exactly. Yeah.

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:08.460
And because they're always one-on-one battles, you know, it doesn't add as much complexity as even once you figure out the battle system in other games, right?

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:17.660
You still have to think about like what each party member is going to do or which monster is best to take out first because like, you know, maybe there's one big strong one and three little rats or something.

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:21.400
And you're like, well, I'm going to take out the three rats first or, you know, maybe the opposite.

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:24.180
This game doesn't have that because it's always one-on-one.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:39.120
So it kind of runs into this thing where you have very little variety until you discover the new enemy, which might just be – it'll have a new name too, but it might just be a new color of like the scorpion or whatever or the drake.

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:52.040
But, you know, once you have those – once you learn how to fight that enemy, as long as you're like monitoring your HP, I didn't find myself changing things up too often, right?

00:20:52.040 --> 00:21:03.640
Like if I was in a situation where if I was up against like something where I realized, oh, I'm in a zone where they expect to be a much higher level than I am, I'll use my magic here because I need to get away from this fight.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.040
But I'd like the experience points.

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:14.700
Whereas if I go back and I'm just dealing with, you know, slimes or whatever, I'm like why not just, you know, attack, attack, attack and just slowly try to whittle away.

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:24.180
I mean there are certain parts – there are certain areas where once you get to a certain level, like every enemy in that area can be taken down with one attack.

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:28.400
So you don't really have to worry about your HP there or your MP there.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:36.500
And you'll just sort of learn that like, okay, in this region, I don't have to worry about anything.

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:40.080
In this one, I might get thrown a curveball with like a magician or something.

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:52.400
And as you move on, like you can do these larger scale strategies of like – it's not like a combat to combat strategy or like I guess when you're in combat.

00:21:52.400 --> 00:22:07.380
But it's more of a planning your trip strategy, like sort of like going from this low-level area to this medium-level area to this low-level area again and just sort of planning what you're going to do for each of those.

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:11.040
You're not really thinking about each individual battle.

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:33.500
Yeah, and the game – to that point, the game I'll say does do a good job of incentivizing you to move on to more difficult areas when you've like outgrown a lower-level area where it's like, yeah, I can take on everyone in this area with no problem and gain the experience and the gold they're giving away.

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:42.220
But all of the prices of everything I need has gone up exponentially enough that it will take me forever to get enough gold if I'm only fighting these guys.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:50.860
So I've got to go take my chances in the place where I'm still in danger and then come back and do that but get a lot more money for putting myself in that danger.

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:57.360
And like that's a basic RPG trope that happens in every RPG I've ever played.

00:22:57.420 --> 00:23:03.040
But I think this is just the first time we've really seen it be so significant to us on the NES.

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:05.300
It's just boiled down and purified to that.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:10.780
Right, right. I was also kind of surprised at the rate of random encounters.

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:17.320
I expected it to be a more like frequent thing, like a nuisance, you know.

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:23.980
It's kind of something that happens in most RPGs is like every three steps an enemy just appears or something.

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:29.680
Sometimes you have to really like walk a while to get a good enemy encounter.

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:30.640
Well, I've found –

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:31.580
Yeah, go ahead.

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:34.180
Sorry. I think you're going to say what I'm about to say but say it.

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:40.180
I found that the only thing I could expect was that it was going to be – it was going to be unexpected.

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:41.940
The amount of times I had to walk.

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:45.600
Like sometimes I would walk three steps like you said and run into an enemy.

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.820
Sometimes I'd walk one step and run into an enemy.

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:51.840
Sometimes I could walk halfway across a continent and not run into an enemy.

00:23:52.040 --> 00:23:55.400
Like it was just completely random.

00:23:56.300 --> 00:24:01.580
Absolutely. Yeah, I felt like I would have long periods of time where one of those things were happening.

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:03.300
Like the game was switching back and forth.

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:07.500
Like, oh, for the next ten minutes, you're not going to run into any – you're going to run into one enemy every minute.

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:10.620
And then, okay, the next ten minutes you're going to run into one enemy every step.

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:18.560
I was just like, how come sometimes it feels – maybe it's the area I'm in, but sometimes it would feel like every step I take I'm in a battle over and over and over again.

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:20.000
And then suddenly that would go away.

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:21.440
Yeah, a little sidebar.

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:25.240
The RNG for this game has been found out.

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:28.200
I watched a speed run that you may or may not have watched.

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:34.760
But this guy games the RNG so that he like basically gets no random encounters.

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:39.080
So it's not – I don't think it's area by area.

00:24:39.340 --> 00:24:44.740
But unless you know to the T what you have to do, it's going to feel random.

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:54.840
Yeah, my understanding was it was actually – and this probably comes in with the RNG as well – is that it depends on the type of tile you're on.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:57.040
That calculates the odds.

00:24:57.340 --> 00:25:03.780
So like the desert has like the highest odds of an enemy spawning with like a 1 in 8 chance.

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:08.080
But most like just regular grass tiles have a 1 in 32 chance.

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:13.040
And I'm not sure if that's just like something that constantly gets re-rolled each step you take.

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:18.540
So like in a field, you just constantly always have a 1 in 32 chance every step you take or not.

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:25.900
Basically what happens is each – like you get a seed like any sort of RNG sort of thing.

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:32.580
And that's based on stuff like your name, the amount of gold you have, the stats that you have.

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:43.840
And then when you're taking – when you're moving like you're – like those odds sort of exist but they're like baked in.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:53.480
Like so if you have the same name and the same amount of gold and if you walk in one direction, you will always hit the same enemy on that same step.

00:25:54.020 --> 00:26:00.960
So if you can manipulate that, then you can sort of see that like it very much is that simple.

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:10.420
But yeah, I mean unless you go that far and like sort of like the odds reset when you stop moving.

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:19.220
So if you – as long as you go that – as long as you don't go that far and you sort of hesitate some, it's going to be random.

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:22.520
But yes, with those odds.

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:51.180
And just talking about the random encounters, it doesn't have to be Dragon Warrior related here.

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:58.340
But I feel like since it's something that we haven't had really yet, what are your guys' thoughts on random encounters in JRPGs anyway?

00:26:58.500 --> 00:27:01.600
Like what is the – some people have a tolerance for this stuff.

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:12.240
Other people would just wish that every battle was either like meaningful or seen on screen like you were able to know that you were about to attack enemies.

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:18.500
Like similar to Chrono Trigger handles it where you can see them on the screen and then you walk up to them and the battle just starts fresh.

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:21.620
You know, Joe, what are your thoughts on random encounters?

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:31.860
Well, I hear a lot of people today talk about how random encounters are outdated and should go away.

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:35.800
And I guess I have a little more of a tolerance for them than that.

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:49.160
But I do find – like if I go back and play – not to bring it back to Final Fantasy, but if I go back and play other Final Fantasy games, old Final Fantasy games that have been like remastered or re-released and they always give you that option to turn off random encounters.

00:27:49.180 --> 00:27:55.260
I do find myself using that a lot and then turning them on when I want to grind and want to gain levels.

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:58.120
So, like maybe I'm a little bit of a hypocrite in that sense.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:09.840
But I like – I like, you know, the idea that if you go at least into like a dungeon that you feel that sense of danger where you're thinking, I need to heal.

00:28:09.920 --> 00:28:13.120
I need to get out of here because danger lurks literally around every corner.

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:15.700
I mean every step I take could present me with an enemy.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:33.360
And on that like note, I usually prefer games that let you have the option to eventually get an ability that helps you or like in this you have what is like fairy water or something that helps that allows you to have the choice to not have random encounters.

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:38.300
Or even in like some Final Fantasy games, you can just get abilities to turn them off like within the gameplay.

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:42.860
I like that because it's kind of like the best of both worlds where it's like you have to kind of earn it.

00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:52.940
You have it. You can turn it on and off and you also get punished for not doing random encounters naturally because if you never do any random encounters, you're going to be underleveled.

00:28:53.240 --> 00:28:58.660
So, I think it's a little more balanced than a lot of people seem to feel these days.

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:00.880
But I mean that's just personal preference.

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:02.900
They can definitely be done wrong.

00:29:03.320 --> 00:29:13.360
Yeah, my ability to tolerate random encounters depends entirely on how much I like the battle system.

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:24.220
Like I can play Final Fantasy 7, 8, 10, like any – I can play those random encounters and not really feel bothered by them unless it's like a Marlboro or something.

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:34.160
Because I genuinely enjoy playing those battles and seeing the spectacle of magic and summons or just like seeing big numbers happen.

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:35.660
Like I enjoy that.

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:44.740
That's satisfying. But there are other RPGs that I don't particularly like the battle system and that usually means I'm not really going to finish the game.

00:29:44.740 --> 00:29:48.240
But it also means that I'd prefer there not to be random encounters.

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:51.240
So, it's just like do I like battling or not?

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:54.220
That's sort of my opinion on it.

00:29:54.220 --> 00:30:08.560
But, you know, one more thing you just made me think of too that I've actually been putting a lot of thought into lately because, you know, a couple months ago I was on a Final Fantasy kick and I replayed 8 and I played 9 for the first time.

00:30:08.680 --> 00:30:11.020
And I picked up 10 for like the 10th time.

00:30:11.020 --> 00:30:17.220
And I love the battle system and the story.

00:30:17.380 --> 00:30:18.700
I'm fascinated by the story of 10.

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:30.460
But the fact that 10 is so linear and I don't get to explore and I don't get to feel like I have agency and I feel like I'm walking a path to get another random encounter to continue on that path to get another random encounter.

00:30:30.820 --> 00:30:36.760
And I have no choice. Like the random encounters feel like a nice complement to like me wanting to explore and go out into danger.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:46.960
When it feels like it's homework where it's like I have nothing to do in between random encounters except for walk until I get another random encounter and hope I run into a boss or a cutscene soon.

00:30:47.240 --> 00:30:55.640
That's why I've never been able to get through Final Fantasy X as much as I love the rest of the ideas of the game is because I just get burnt out on it after a while.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:31:05.100
Like I feel like random encounters work really well if your gameplay loop is keeping me captivated otherwise.

00:31:05.100 --> 00:31:14.840
I sort of think like even though it's the opposite problem of like this very linear game where you're waiting for cutscenes and bosses.

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:16.600
But it sort of is the same.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:24.200
It's the same problem too with Dragon Warrior because unless you know where to go you are just sort of.

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:28.180
And yes you're exploring but you're exploring very similar environments.

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:30.740
Yeah with nothing to do with nothing to do.

00:31:30.740 --> 00:31:35.980
You're just sort of walking around getting into random encounters and then you might take two steps and get into another random encounter.

00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:37.700
So you're right.

00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:49.820
Like it is it's a really ticky tacky thing where I don't really know what if I can have a definitive answer about these things.

00:31:49.820 --> 00:32:01.080
I was surprised even just to see Dragon Warrior have options though like there's the repel spell so that you can walk a certain amount of steps without even getting into an encounter.

00:32:01.360 --> 00:32:14.540
But also I forget it might be something you wear or an item you get but that basically anything that's you know less powerful than you will no longer be something that you randomly encounter.

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:19.480
Like if your defense stat is higher than its attack stat you just don't run into it anymore.

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:29.820
And I was surprised to see something like that so early on because I think those kinds of solutions help make it so that you're not constantly just fighting blue slimes all over the map.

00:32:30.160 --> 00:32:34.480
Now granted they shouldn't be placing them in areas other than the beginning anyway.

00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:47.260
But speaking of just placing enemies it's really funny if you for some reason do feel like exploring very early on there is a way to run into a series of I guess they're they're hills because the mountains you can't walk over.

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:49.540
There's hills at like the southern end of the map.

00:32:49.640 --> 00:32:50.680
Yeah. That have scorpions.

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:52.240
I ran into that. Yeah.

00:32:52.460 --> 00:32:56.200
And it's like you're just screwed if you're like level two and you're trying to fight scorpions.

00:32:56.200 --> 00:33:09.780
Yeah. You have I feel like you have a couple of options of places to go that you will just be completely outmatched early on which is nice to come back to later and see that you've progressed and see you can just kick anything's ass that comes at you.

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:15.020
But sometimes it feels a little not particularly balanced.

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:17.280
Yeah. So I don't know.

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:24.400
I mean do I prefer the games where the JRPGs where it's just like the enemies are out there and they endlessly spawn right.

00:33:24.500 --> 00:33:32.740
It's not like you there's a finite amount of experience to have but like they're just out there in the overworld and you have to run up to them to to start the fight.

00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:34.500
You know maybe I prefer that.

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:48.300
But I also think that sometimes there's a surprise to like OK well I have 14 HP left and the town is over there and I think I can get in one more battle for experience points because I want to save my gold at the end.

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:52.780
And it's like don't get greedy like you might just want to check into the town and call it a day.

00:33:53.240 --> 00:34:00.240
I do like that and that's something that you could completely avoid in games where all the all the battles are conveyed to you on screen.

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:04.200
Yeah. I got one example and this will be the last thing I say on the matter.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:12.360
Is Persona 5 like that is the only modern JRPG that I've played in the last like five years.

00:34:13.900 --> 00:34:22.140
And that is a game where your enemies are they just show up on like the dungeon like while you're playing when you're when you're walking around.

00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:23.420
And I don't know.

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:24.760
I kind of didn't like it.

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:29.980
I maybe it's just the old school in me of like the old school Final Fantasy stuff.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:35.020
But I I it just felt wrong.

00:34:35.020 --> 00:34:35.920
I don't know.

00:34:36.800 --> 00:34:40.140
I don't know.

00:34:40.160 --> 00:34:50.260
I don't know.

00:35:04.320 --> 00:35:07.360
now is appropriate to talk about since we're talking about the grinding and stuff like that.

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:11.180
You said that the game isn't story locked.

00:35:11.260 --> 00:35:14.360
It's just level locked in the sense that you'll go to

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:20.100
a new area and quickly realize that you've been outmatched and you can't be exploring here because

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:22.040
there's a high probability that you'll die.

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:25.220
So you go back to the earlier part of the area that

00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:26.040
you were hanging out in.

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:30.100
You grind there for as long as necessary to either buy the new weapon or

00:35:30.100 --> 00:35:31.120
level up your stats.

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:35.740
But I can't help but notice that these area gateways, right, of like new enemies

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:41.600
being introduced to you, it's not just like, oh, I'm level four and I need to be level five.

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:44.900
A lot of times it's like two to three levels per area.

00:35:45.020 --> 00:35:45.960
Would you guys agree with that?

00:35:46.060 --> 00:35:47.000
Like it takes a

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:50.360
load of grinding to move on, not just one level.

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:52.480
It's not very granular.

00:35:53.880 --> 00:35:55.180
They're pretty high jumps.

00:35:55.180 --> 00:36:02.960
So you will have to grind or mindlessly explore for a long time before you can go from one to the

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:04.560
other. Yeah.

00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:11.540
And I also personally felt that my level, I had a harder time figuring out how much

00:36:11.540 --> 00:36:16.840
my level was contributing because as I was going up level, I was getting more gold and buying better

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:20.840
armor. And I felt like the, I was armor and weapons and shields.

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:22.540
And I felt like those are what really

00:36:22.540 --> 00:36:24.060
felt like they were making the difference to me.

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:25.740
And I'm sure the level, you know, obviously it was

00:36:25.740 --> 00:36:31.600
increasing my attack and defense as well, but it felt like the armors, like getting enough gold to

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:35.640
get the new armor was when I felt comfortable to like, oh, now I'll go explore that area because

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:41.460
this armor is, is making it significantly easier or the sword is making it one hit kills for all these

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:42.640
enemies that used to give me trouble.

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:44.860
Yeah, I definitely felt that too.

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:50.960
And the way the leveling up works too, I agree with you there, Joe, because the reward between

00:36:50.960 --> 00:36:56.380
each level does feel kind of small unless you're getting a new magic spell, in which case then it's

00:36:56.380 --> 00:37:00.160
like, oh, cool. Like I should try this out in combat or see what it does, blah, blah, blah.

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:06.240
And, you know, I see if it's worth incorporating into my, um, my battles with the current enemies that I'm

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:12.300
dealing with. But, you know, leveling up in general is a weird thing because, uh, similar to all the other

00:37:12.300 --> 00:37:16.580
RNG stuff we're talking, we were talking about, like it's your name is the first four letters of

00:37:16.580 --> 00:37:20.300
your name are like what tie your stats that you level up on.

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:22.340
And like, so certain names have like

00:37:22.340 --> 00:37:25.020
better strength and other ones level up defense.

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:29.020
So like, yeah, I would, I would argue that the

00:37:29.020 --> 00:37:36.700
leveling up aspect, like going from level four to five meant less to me than, uh, changing from a

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:37.720
club to a sword.

00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:40.900
Yeah. Specifically because like, you know, those are also giving you like,

00:37:40.900 --> 00:37:46.560
you know, the club does plus two attack damage and the sword, I'm making this all up.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:48.760
I don't know, but this, and the sword does plus five.

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:51.300
So you're immediately getting a plus three stat boost

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:55.680
that a level up might not necessarily give you depending on what name you picked.

00:37:55.960 --> 00:38:00.220
Yeah. I didn't realize that the RNG, uh, you guys were talking about also would,

00:38:00.380 --> 00:38:04.260
I didn't know that it affected your experience, but, um, that is crazy.

00:38:04.360 --> 00:38:05.620
I didn't know that. Yeah.

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:07.080
There's some weird name combos.

00:38:07.300 --> 00:38:11.540
Like if you do, um, link, uh, you know, like legend of

00:38:11.540 --> 00:38:17.600
Zelda link, uh, apparently you have, um, like great strength stats, but bad defensive stats.

00:38:17.660 --> 00:38:19.540
And I'm not sure why that would be the case.

00:38:19.600 --> 00:38:21.840
I feel like, you know, it's probably just an error.

00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:24.840
Like people, people probably would tell you that like, if you put in this guy's name,

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:30.760
he has great this and great that, but there is, um, if you put test T E S T in all capitals

00:38:30.760 --> 00:38:37.200
as your name, you do start off with the best possible stats, um, to, for the game.

00:38:37.520 --> 00:38:44.460
Granted, all of these best possible stats things, like it's not like, um, it's like level 1.5, right?

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:48.160
Right. By the time you get to level 15, it doesn't even really matter anymore.

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:53.100
Like what your name was, but it does matter early on, which I think is a strange decision to make,

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:56.340
but you can also choose Enix who is the, um, the company.

00:38:56.680 --> 00:38:59.700
And I think they, uh, also give you like,

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:01.660
uh, amazing defense or something like that.

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:03.740
So there is some fun to be had with the names too.

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:04.840
So try out some other ones.

00:39:06.820 --> 00:39:08.100
I'll start my playthrough over.

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:09.580
There you go. There you go.

00:39:09.800 --> 00:39:13.140
As XJZ exclamation point.

00:39:14.180 --> 00:39:16.100
X, X, isn't that like a Nickelodeon?

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:17.520
My life is a teenage robot.

00:39:18.380 --> 00:39:20.680
XJ9. I've never heard of what you're talking about.

00:39:21.020 --> 00:39:22.160
It's a great show, Sean.

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:23.620
It's not, it's not new either.

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:27.160
It's, it's from, uh, maybe like, maybe we were like slightly,

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:30.600
slightly getting to the point where we were too old to watch that show, but you know,

00:39:30.700 --> 00:39:34.960
I still caught it here and there, you know, or at least me, I, me and Sean are a little older than you,

